{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1550081690,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0MDk5MzYwNDM=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3162","origin":"github"},"title":"run: multistage mode of operation","message":"I think with `reproman run` or without it would be nice to be able to do something like \n`datalad run --input ... --output ... --stages=prepare,exec sbatch --array 0-100  script.sbatch`\nso it would do everything needed (`prepare` - get/unlock; may be worth separate even more?), and `exec` (choose word different from `run`), but not even try to save since that is a batch job and no relevant changes would be present upon sbatch completion.  I also do not want to make it into some wrapper which would stay there waiting for that queued job to complete. I want manually, whenever it is done, do `datalad run --stages=save` which would pick up from the spot it left.\nThe initial implementation could be minimal, just storing some `.git/DATALAD_RUN` file with e.g. the json record upon initial execution, to be later reused in every stage, and later saved in a commit. `datalad run` without --stages could whine/crash if it runs into such record.\nEven without guarding for which states were actually already executed, allowing to hurt yourself,  it would open up a number of usecases for `datalad run`.\n\nIdeally, of cause, the final json record should have some list of stages which were executed in separate `run` invocations, and we would need to be able to pass e.g. `exit_status` to the `save` stage.\n\nWhat do you think @kyleam or may be there is already some way to do it? e.g. may be at least exposing `inject` parameter of `run_command` then, I guess, I could rerun original command in full twice (2nd time with `--inject`?","files":null}]}